<section class="section" aria-labelledby="creator-title">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="about-grid">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="creator-title">Who is behind Brazil Decoded?</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p class="section-note">
          Brazil Decoded was created by a Brazilian who grew up here, still lives here,
          and has spent years watching foreign visitors navigate the country —
          what confuses them, what catches them off guard, and what nobody warned them about.
        </p>
        <p class="section-note">
          The idea started simply: too many tourists were arriving with the wrong picture of Brazil.
          Not because they hadn't researched — but because most of what's published
          either oversells the experience or amplifies fears that don't reflect everyday reality.
        </p>
        <p class="section-note">
          This guide was built to fix that.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tinted" aria-labelledby="purpose-title">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="about-grid">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="purpose-title">Why this Brazil travel guide exists</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p class="section-note">
          Travel content about Brazil tends to fall into one of two traps:
          it either glosses over real challenges, or it turns minor risks into dramatic warnings.
          Neither version actually prepares you.
        </p>
        <p class="section-note">
          Brazil Decoded was created to fill the space in between —
          the practical middle ground that most guides skip:
        </p>
        <ul class="mini-list">
          <li>how people actually behave on the street, in restaurants, on public transport</li>
          <li>which situations carry real risk — and which ones don't</li>
          <li>cultural signals that locals read naturally but visitors miss</li>
          <li>how to handle money, transport, and communication without confusion</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="section-note">
          The goal isn't to sell you on Brazil. It's to make sure you arrive knowing what to expect.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="experience-title">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="about-grid">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="experience-title">Built on daily life, not research trips</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p class="section-note">
          The content here doesn't come from a two-week press trip or a compilation of other blogs.
          It comes from someone who lives in Brazil and has watched foreign visitors
          navigate the country — across different cities, situations, and seasons.
        </p>
        <p class="section-note">
          That means the guide reflects:
        </p>
        <ul class="mini-list">
          <li>the unwritten rules of public spaces that no one posts signs about</li>
          <li>how locals actually handle money — and why tourists sometimes get caught out</li>
          <li>what behavior invites attention, and what makes you invisible</li>
          <li>how to read situations the way someone who grew up here would</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="section-note">
          This is the kind of context that's hard to find anywhere else —
          because most people who have it aren't writing travel guides.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tinted" aria-labelledby="audience-title">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="about-grid">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="audience-title">Who this guide was written for</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        <p class="section-note">
          Brazil Decoded is written specifically for:
        </p>
        <ul class="mini-list">
          <li>first-time visitors who want to arrive informed, not anxious</li>
          <li>travelers who do their research before landing — not after something goes wrong</li>
          <li>people who want to understand the place, not just pass through it</li>
          <li>anyone who values calm, respectful travel over performance travel</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="section-note">
          If you're looking for nightlife recommendations or luxury hotel lists,
          this isn't the right guide.
          But if you want to feel genuinely prepared — for the ordinary, the unexpected,
          and everything in between — you're in the right place.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

